Call a function by writing its name followed by arguments.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    result = double(21)
]

Arguments are positional by default. They can also name their parameter:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    label = describe(subject = "apple")
]

Positional arguments come first. Named arguments come afterward.

Mutable parameters need explicit access to an existing mutable value:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count ~= 1
    increment(~count)
    increment(value = ~count)
]

For a named argument, put `~` before the value. Fresh values and independent
copies don't use `~`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    increment(1)
    increment(copy count)
]

Ordinary function calls keep their usual syntax, including empty `f()`. Directives
share the call rules but reject an authored empty `()`; see @./ (Calls and access advanced).
